What I liked:

  Your slides were clean and attractive. The explicit mention of
  the two constraints. The balance infographic on slide 19. Siva
  Lakshmi and Priyansh's presentations. The exponential decay
  infographic on slide 22.


What can be improved:

  1) When you are giving an overview of the entire project (right
  at the beginning) the visual still shows the title
  page. Visuals should be in sync with the idea being presented.

  2) Defining U = {1,...,|U|} does not make sense, you are
  defining U using U.

  3) Always start with a simple toy numerical example, where you
  show the increases of the counts for a given short input.

  4) Never use a technical term before introducing it first (may
  be informally). Terms like bucket and CMS table came before the
  audience had any chance to guess their meanings!

  5) The "approximation bargain" idea is well presented, but it
  came before the audience even had a flavour of the algorithm!
  An initial numerical example would have solved this problem
  easily.

  6) It was clear Manogna was reading the slides prepared by
  others, without knowing (and even more seriously without
  caring) what the things meant! Such a presentation is a niearly
  sure recipe of ruining one's career. Even if you do not care
  about the slides, you should at least care about your career!  

  7) Slide 12 should have come much earlier along with an
  example.

  8) The infographic motivating taking the minimum is not very
  clear.

  9) Slide 14 could be presented as puzzles to the audience (Of
  course, this is not very suitable for a hybrid presentation).

  10) When you are presenting lots of things in a single slide,
  reveal it step by step. Most presentation softwares have some
  way or other to achieve this.

  11) Rishikesh should have started with anumerical
  example. Algebraic symbold are hard to follow during a talk. 

  12) Avoid using terms that you cannot explain, like
  "armortized", "L1/L2 cache" at on slide 23.

  13) Once you were comparing two sizes, one being 9000 times
  smaller than the other. Yet the inforgraphic showed something
  like 6 times smaller. Too much off scale! 
